{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-270888",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{6}{x-2} \\\\ y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{x-2} & =-4 \\\\ 6 & =-4x+8 \\\\ -2 & =-4x \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since the line is $y=-4,$ the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point is $-4.$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $A=(\\,\\frac{1}{2},-4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14267214/assets/d8a365b1247b0a2dca158b26042b04e4e98c0eaacce0f1a2392cec389e0e230c.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
